common-passwords.txt: where it comes from, and its licence
============================================================

What it is: the passwords PM OS refuses as common (docs/password-sign-in.md, "The rules for
a password"; crates/toolserver/src/password/hash.rs, `is_common`). One per line, lower-case,
most common first, 46,146 lines.

Source: SecLists, by Daniel Miessler and contributors,
  https://github.com/danielmiessler/SecLists
  file Passwords/Common-Credentials/xato-net-10-million-passwords-1000000.txt
  (the 1,000,000 most frequent passwords of Mark Burnett's ten-million-password data set,
  as published by xato.net), fetched from the repository's master branch on 2026-11-21.
  SHA-256 of the fetched file:
  424a3e03a17df0a2bc2b3ca749d81b04e79d59cb7aeec8876a5a3f308d0caf51

How it was derived: every entry of that file, trimmed and lower-cased (PM OS lower-cases and
trims a new password before the check), kept when it is 12 to 128 characters long (shorter or
longer ones are already refused by the length rule, so listing them would change nothing),
first occurrence only, in the source's order. Nothing was added or edited by hand. The entries
are data: some are offensive words, as in the source.
  SHA-256 of common-passwords.txt:
  463363d6be260dec0b2bb9cbad7a808811cfadc55436afb245312f3c336528a1

To refresh it: fetch the same file again, apply the same rule, and update both hashes here.

